{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01MZqaBGWAoNpqfRG5XWMnMD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential verification of the Rust translation against `c_src`\n2\t\n3\tThe C program is the ground truth. Both programs were built and then run as\n4\tsubprocesses on the same inputs; **stdout, stderr and exit status were compared\n5\tbyte for byte / value for value** (the exit status is compared as the raw\n6\t`wait(2)` status, so a death by signal would also show up).\n7\t\n8\t```\n9\t# C  (ground truth)\n10\tcd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver\n11\t# Rust\n12\tcd translation && cargo build --release                                # -> translation/target/release/driver\n13\t# the suite (builds the C binary itself if it is missing)\n14\tcd translation && cargo test --release\n15\t```\n16\t\n17\t`translation/tests/differential.rs` drives the two **binaries** (never the Rust\n18\tcode as a library) through `std::process::Command`, with raw-byte `argv` and\n19\traw-byte stdin.\n20\t\n21\t## Result\n22\t\n23\t**One real mismatch was found and fixed** (the `SIGPIPE` exit status, §2 below);\n24\t**no mismatch remains in the translated logic.** 24 test functions covering\n25\troughly 5,500 distinct input cases (≈11,000 process launches) all agree — in\n26\tboth the `--release` and the default (overflow-checked) profile — and additional\n27\tthrow-away sweeps run during the investigation (≈40,000 more cases, including\n28\t40,000 pseudo-random `i32` values folded through `classify`/`process_stream`)\n29\talso agree.\n30\t\n31\tBranch enumeration was checked mechanically: a `gcov`-instrumented copy of the\n32\tC sources (compiled *outside* `c_src`, which was never modified) reports\n33\t**100 % of the 234 executable C lines executed** by the enumerated case list.\n34\tThe only never-taken branches left are unreachable ones:\n35\t\n36\t* `if (e && ...)` in `main.c` — `strtol` never leaves `e == NULL`;\n37\t* `iv_reserve`'s `nc > SIZE_MAX/2` and `!p` (realloc failure) paths;\n38\t* `if (acc > 0x7fffffffLL)` in `process_a_stream` — would need ~3·10⁸ stream\n39\t  elements (see trap 3 below: the *second* clamp always fires anyway).\n40\t\n41\t## Mismatches actually observed\n42\t\n43\t### 1. `--help` prints `argv[0]`, so the two binaries print their own paths\n44\t\n45\t`usage()` does `fprintf(stderr, \"Usage: %s [--stdin] ...\", argv[0])`. Run from\n46\ttheir build locations, the C binary prints\n47\t`Usage: .../c_src/build/driver ...` and the Rust binary prints\n48\t`Usage: .../translation/target/release/driver ...`.\n49\t\n50\t* Cause: the value of `argv[0]`, i.e. where each executable lives — **not** a\n51\t  translation defect. There is nothing in the Rust to fix: it faithfully echoes\n52\t  `argv[0]`.\n53\t* Consequence for testing: a naive comparison of `--help` \"fails\" for a reason\n54\t  that has nothing to do with the translation. The harness therefore forces\n55\t  `argv[0]` to the literal `driver` for **both** programs\n56\t  (`CommandExt::arg0(\"driver\")`), after which `--help` is byte-identical. This\n57\t  was confirmed independently with `exec -a driver ...` in a shell.\n58\t\n59\t### 2. Wrong exit status when stdout is a pipe with no reader (FIXED)\n60\t\n61\t* Symptom: with a program whose output exceeds the 64 KiB pipe buffer and a\n62\t  reader that goes away early, e.g.\n63\t  `driver 0 1 7 300000 3 | head -c 4`, the **C is killed by `SIGPIPE`**\n64\t  (raw wait status 13, shell exit 141) while the Rust build **exited 0**.\n65\t  Reproduced with a reader that reads 4 bytes and closes the pipe:\n66\t  C `-13`, Rust `0`.\n67\t* Cause: the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, and\n68\t  every write in the translation discards its `Result` (`let _ = write!(...)`,\n69\t  matching C's habit of ignoring `printf`'s return). A C program starts with the\n70\t  default `SIGPIPE` disposition and therefore dies. Nothing in the translated\n71\t  *logic* was wrong — the difference came from the Rust runtime's startup.\n72\t* Fix: `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls\n73\t  `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first thing `main` does. Normal runs (stdout\n74\t  captured to a file or a fully-read pipe) are unaffected; only the\n75\t  closed-reader case changes, and it now matches C exactly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc26c2df-0846-4744-a03c-650c845acfb6","uuid":"9bff596c-edef-4eb0-b5e9-489219c10b7f","timestamp":"2026-08-28T07:13:08.272Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-CgkIkg/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# Differential verification of the Rust translation against `c_src`\n\nThe C program is the ground truth. Both programs were built and then run as\nsubprocesses on the same inputs; **stdout, stderr and exit status were compared\nbyte for byte / value for value** (the exit status is compared as the raw\n`wait(2)` status, so a death by signal would also show up).\n\n```\n# C  (ground truth)\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver\n# Rust\ncd translation && cargo build --release                                # -> translation/target/release/driver\n# the suite (builds the C binary itself if it is missing)\ncd translation && cargo test --release\n```\n\n`translation/tests/differential.rs` drives the two **binaries** (never the Rust\ncode as a library) through `std::process::Command`, with raw-byte `argv` and\nraw-byte stdin.\n\n## Result\n\n**One real mismatch was found and fixed** (the `SIGPIPE` exit status, §2 below);\n**no mismatch remains in the translated logic.** 24 test functions covering\nroughly 5,500 distinct input cases (≈11,000 process launches) all agree — in\nboth the `--release` and the default (overflow-checked) profile — and additional\nthrow-away sweeps run during the investigation (≈40,000 more cases, including\n40,000 pseudo-random `i32` values folded through `classify`/`process_stream`)\nalso agree.\n\nBranch enumeration was checked mechanically: a `gcov`-instrumented copy of the\nC sources (compiled *outside* `c_src`, which was never modified) reports\n**100 % of the 234 executable C lines executed** by the enumerated case list.\nThe only never-taken branches left are unreachable ones:\n\n* `if (e && ...)` in `main.c` — `strtol` never leaves `e == NULL`;\n* `iv_reserve`'s `nc > SIZE_MAX/2` and `!p` (realloc failure) paths;\n* `if (acc > 0x7fffffffLL)` in `process_a_stream` — would need ~3·10⁸ stream\n  elements (see trap 3 below: the *second* clamp always fires anyway).\n\n## Mismatches actually observed\n\n### 1. `--help` prints `argv[0]`, so the two binaries print their own paths\n\n`usage()` does `fprintf(stderr, \"Usage: %s [--stdin] ...\", argv[0])`. Run from\ntheir build locations, the C binary prints\n`Usage: .../c_src/build/driver ...` and the Rust binary prints\n`Usage: .../translation/target/release/driver ...`.\n\n* Cause: the value of `argv[0]`, i.e. where each executable lives — **not** a\n  translation defect. There is nothing in the Rust to fix: it faithfully echoes\n  `argv[0]`.\n* Consequence for testing: a naive comparison of `--help` \"fails\" for a reason\n  that has nothing to do with the translation. The harness therefore forces\n  `argv[0]` to the literal `driver` for **both** programs\n  (`CommandExt::arg0(\"driver\")`), after which `--help` is byte-identical. This\n  was confirmed independently with `exec -a driver ...` in a shell.\n\n### 2. Wrong exit status when stdout is a pipe with no reader (FIXED)\n\n* Symptom: with a program whose output exceeds the 64 KiB pipe buffer and a\n  reader that goes away early, e.g.\n  `driver 0 1 7 300000 3 | head -c 4`, the **C is killed by `SIGPIPE`**\n  (raw wait status 13, shell exit 141) while the Rust build **exited 0**.\n  Reproduced with a reader that reads 4 bytes and closes the pipe:\n  C `-13`, Rust `0`.\n* Cause: the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, and\n  every write in the translation discards its `Result` (`let _ = write!(...)`,\n  matching C's habit of ignoring `printf`'s return). A C program starts with the\n  default `SIGPIPE` disposition and therefore dies. Nothing in the translated\n  *logic* was wrong — the difference came from the Rust runtime's startup.\n* Fix: `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls\n  `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first thing `main` does. Normal runs (stdout\n  captured to a file or a fully-read pipe) are unaffected; only the\n  closed-reader case changes, and it now matches C exactly.","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":212}}}
